 "chapter": "18",
 "title": "Occurrence, Preparation, and Compounds of Oxygen",
 "short": "Oxygen",
 "lead": "Oxygen, the most abundant element in the earth’s crust, is separated from liquid air and reacts with nearly every element. The section takes up <span data-concept=\"ozone\">ozone</span>, the <span data-concept=\"oxide\">oxides</span>, <span data-concept=\"peroxide\">peroxides</span>, <span data-concept=\"superoxide\">superoxides</span> and <span data-concept=\"hydroxide\">hydroxides</span> of the representative metals with the <span data-concept=\"chlor-alkali-process\">chlor-alkali process</span>, and the nonmetal oxides and the <span data-concept=\"oxyacid\">oxyacids</span> of nitrogen, phosphorus, sulfur and the halogens with their <span data-concept=\"salt\">salts</span>.",
 "objectives": [
  "Describe the properties, preparation, and compounds of oxygen",
  "Describe the preparation, properties, and uses of some representative metal oxides, peroxides, and hydroxides"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>Oxygen is one of the most reactive elements. This reactivity, coupled with its abundance, makes the chemistry of oxygen very rich and well understood.</p><p>Compounds of the representative metals with oxygen exist in three categories (1) <span data-concept=\"oxide\">oxides</span>, (2) <span data-concept=\"peroxide\">peroxides</span> and <span data-concept=\"superoxide\">superoxides</span>, and (3) <span data-concept=\"hydroxide\">hydroxides</span>. Heating the corresponding hydroxides, <span data-concept=\"nitrate\">nitrates</span>, or <span data-concept=\"carbonate\">carbonates</span> is the most common method for producing oxides. Heating the metal or metal oxide in oxygen may lead to the formation of peroxides and superoxides. The soluble oxides dissolve in water to form solutions of hydroxides. Most metals oxides are <span data-concept=\"base-anhydride\">base anhydrides</span> and react with <span data-concept=\"acid\">acids</span>. The hydroxides of the representative metals react with acids in <span data-concept=\"acid-base-reaction\">acid-base reactions</span> to form <span data-concept=\"salt\">salts</span> and water. The hydroxides have many commercial uses.</p><p>All nonmetals except fluorine form multiple oxides. Nearly all of the nonmetal oxides are <span data-concept=\"acid-anhydride\">acid anhydrides</span>. <span data-concept=\"oxyacid-structure-and-strength\">The acidity of oxyacids requires that the hydrogen atoms bond to the oxygen atoms in the molecule rather than to the other nonmetal atom</span>. Generally, the strength of the oxyacid increases with the number of oxygen atoms bonded to the nonmetal atom and not to a hydrogen.</p>",
 "notes": "The Link to Learning video of gold dissolving in aqua regia is left out, and the section keeps as printed “2HCL”, the unbalanced Na₂O(s) + H₂O(l) ⟶ NaOH(aq), “Tl₂O(s) + H₂O(aq)”, “peroxides ions”, the comma after “a bond order of 1½”, “tin(IV), SnO₂, and lead(IV), PbO₂”, “few second- and third-row transition metals”, “The process known as the chlor-alkali process, utilizes”, “Nitrogen pentaoxide”, “hydrogen phosphate ion, HPO₃²⁻” for hydrogen phosphite, the Δ over the arrow of 2H₂SO₃ + O₂, K<sub>a</sub> = 1.2 × 10⁻² for both HSO₄⁻ and the first step of H₂SO₃, “2+-oxidation state”, “7+-oxidation state”, the subscripted comma after H₂S, the summary’s “Most metals oxides” and the key’s “the oxygen electron”.",
